From: ms178 toolchain patchset v15 (v14 + signed integer division /
 modulo by a loop-invariant divisor)
Subject: [PATCH v15] GCC 16.1.1 Raptor Lake (i7-14700KF) performance patchset,
 rebased onto bcda9a5a1d995c1a0fe1fb64bfd4dddd44224ba5

Downstream performance patchset for GCC 16.1.1 (BASE-VER 16.1.1), tuned
for Intel Raptor Lake (i7-14700KF) and the workloads I actually build:
Mesa/RADV/ACO, Qt 6, xorg-xwayland, xlibre-server.

Applies with ZERO fuzz, ZERO offsets, ZERO rejects onto
bcda9a5a1d995c1a0fe1fb64bfd4dddd44224ba5:

    patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run -i ms178-1.patch   =>  clean
    git apply --check ms178-1.patch            =>  clean

=====================================================================
WHAT IS NEW IN v15
=====================================================================

Signed integer division and modulo by a loop-invariant divisor are now
strength-reduced as well.  v14 shipped the unsigned case and explicitly
declined signed; the reason it is cheap to add is that truncating signed
division is just the unsigned division of the absolute values plus a
sign fixup, and |n| / |d| always fit the unsigned type of the same
width - even for the minimum value.

  preheader:  sd = d >> (N-1)          // 0 or -1
              |d| = (d ^ sd) - sd      // magic number built from this
  body:       sn = n >> (N-1)
              |n| = (n ^ sn) - sn
              uq  = <the v14 unsigned sequence applied to |n|>
              s   = sn ^ sd
              q   = (uq ^ s) - s

Both undefined signed divisors stay total in the preheader, which
matters because the preheader executes even on iterations the original
program would never have run: d == 0 is masked exactly as in the
unsigned path, and d == -1 gives |d| == 1, i.e. the identity path, so
INT_MIN / -1 wraps to INT_MIN rather than raising SIGFPE.  Signed modulo
recovers the remainder as n - q * d from the *original* signed operands,
computed in the unsigned type where wrapping is well defined.

Measured on the development host, 65536 elements, best of 11 runs.  The
control is the identical compiler with -fno-tree-loop-invdiv, so the
only difference is this pass:

    loop              control        v15        speedup
    ----------------------------------------------------
    signed 32 div    1.7650 ns    0.2238 ns      7.9x
    signed 32 mod    1.7650 ns    0.2987 ns      5.9x
    signed 64 div    2.9414 ns    1.3170 ns      2.2x
    unsigned 32 div  1.7650 ns    0.1337 ns     13.2x
    unsigned 32 mod  1.7650 ns    0.1921 ns      9.2x
    unsigned 64 div  2.9414 ns    0.8833 ns      3.3x

Correctness was established by exhaustion rather than by argument:
signed N=8 and N=16 over every (d, n) pair, 4,319,032,822 checks, zero
mismatches, quotient and remainder both verified, with the two genuinely
undefined combinations excluded from the reference only.  N=32 and N=64
were covered by a structured plus randomized sweep: every small divisor
of both signs, every 2^i and 2^i +- 1, the type extremes, and
quotient-boundary numerators k*d-1, k*d, k*d+1.

=====================================================================
CONTENTS - all 22 changes in this patchset
=====================================================================

Vectorizer cost model and dependence analysis
---------------------------------------------

 1-13. gcc/tree-vect-loop.cc, gcc/tree-vect-data-refs.cc: thirteen
       defects of one class - 32-bit or signed/unsigned arithmetic on
       quantities that are neither bounded to 32 bits nor
       unsigned-safe.  Affects vect_estimate_min_profitable_iters and
       the dependence-distance computations, where truncation produced
       wrong costs and wrong distances.

 14.   gcc/tree-vect-data-refs.cc, vect_peeling_hash_choose_best_peeling:
       wrong sentinel value made the best-peeling search skip a
       candidate.

 15.   gcc/tree-vect-data-refs.cc, vect_peeling_supportable: DR_STEP was
       kept in an unsigned int, truncating large or negative steps.

 19.   gcc/tree-vect-patterns.cc, vect_recog_bitfield_ref_pattern: the
       pattern only fired for CONVERT or gcond consumers, so a
       BIT_FIELD_REF feeding BIT_AND or a compare stayed in the IL and
       SLP rejected the whole region ("BIT_FIELD_REF not supported").
       Now the BIT_FIELD_REF assignment itself is matched.  On ACO's
       Temp accessors this is 3.8x on size() and 2.0x on type() and
       is_linear(); aco_register_allocation.cpp gains 102 vector insns.

 20.   gcc/tree-scalar-evolution.cc: SCEV handled LSHIFT_EXPR but had no
       RSHIFT_EXPR case, so any right-shifted induction variable was
       opaque.  Added, guarded by the step being a multiple of 2^C and
       by a no-wrap proof.  Both guards are load-bearing: without the
       first, a brute-force search finds 221,520,100 violations, and
       the no-wrap proof must be passed rhs1 rather than NULL_TREE or
       two of its three cases are dead code.  mesa_bytes_to_hex goes
       25.3 -> 22.9 ns, about 10%.

x86 code generation
-------------------

 16.   gcc/expmed.cc, store_bit_field_1: insert a whole sub-vector via
       vec_init instead of element by element.  Eight insns become one
       vinserti128.  21 insertion shapes validated.

 17.   gcc/config/i386/i386.md: the MULX highpart pattern introduced in
       v10 was dead code - a '*'-named define_insn is only reachable if
       something else already built that RTL.  Turned
       <s>mul<mode>3_highpart into a define_expand emitting a
       non-starred bmi2_umul<mode>3_highpart, and extended the u_bool
       code attribute accordingly.  Three insns become two.

 18.   gcc/tree-ssa-phiopt.cc: the non-IEEE MIN/MAX fallback rejected
       the four UNORDERED comparison codes, so Mesa's CLAMP and
       SATURATE macros (572 call sites across 150 files) emitted
       vucomiss + vmovd + cmova + vmovd instead of a single vminss.
       UNGE/UNGT/UNLE/UNLT are now normalised to LT/LE/GT/GE with the
       arms swapped.  Only observable with -fno-trapping-math, which
       these builds use.  1.95x on a saturate loop.

 (1)   gcc/expmed.cc: use vec_set even when the stored field's mode
       differs from the vector element mode.
 (2)   gcc/config/i386/i386-expand.cc: V2DI/V2DF zero-lane insertion.
 (3)   gcc/config/i386/sse.md: accept reg_or_0_operand where a zero
       lane is being written.
 (4)   gcc/simplify-rtx.cc: fuse nested VEC_MERGEs that share an
       operand.

Graphite
--------

 21.   gcc/graphite-optimize-isl.cc, gcc/graphite-isl-ast-to-gimple.cc:
       two state-leak fixes.  These matter here specifically because
       the Mesa, Qt and X server CFLAGS carry -fgraphite-identity and
       -floop-nest-optimize, so every translation unit goes through
       Graphite.

       (a) Sticky ISL error.  graphite_transform_loops allocates one
           isl_ctx and shares it across every SCoP in the function, and
           isl_ctx_last_error persists until explicitly reset.
           build_poly_scop knew this; optimize_isl and scop_to_isl_ast
           did not.  So the first SCoP to exhaust --param
           max-isl-operations, or to hit any other ISL error, poisoned
           the shared context and every later SCoP bailed out reading a
           stale error - one hard loop nest disabled Graphite for the
           whole rest of the function.  Both sites now read the error
           into a local, reset immediately, and test the local.

       (b) Leaked global scheduler options.  optimize_isl set six
           global isl_ctx options and restored none of them, while
           carefully restoring on_error and max_operations.  All six
           are now saved and restored on every exit path.

       Demonstration: with two SCoPs and --param
       max-isl-operations=20000, the baseline optimizes zero loop nests
       and the fixed compiler optimizes the second one.

Loop optimization
-----------------

 22.   gcc/gimple-loop-invdiv.cc (new pass, -ftree-loop-invdiv, on by
       default at -O2 and above except with -Os): replace integer
       division or modulo by a loop-invariant but non-constant divisor
       with a reciprocal multiply, computing the magic number once in
       the loop preheader.

       The formulation deliberately needs no runtime test and no loop
       duplication, because it is correct for every divisor value
       including 1 and including every power of two.  That is what
       makes it strictly better than versioning the loop for a
       power-of-two divisor: versioning doubles the loop, needs an
       unpredictable branch, and in branchless form is an outright
       pessimization (shrx + divl + cmove - the division still runs).

       The pass is placed inside pass_tree_loop after pass_loop_split
       and before the vectorizer.  This is deliberate and is where most
       of the win comes from: a division cannot be vectorized at all,
       whereas the MULT_HIGHPART sequence can, so the loop does not
       merely lose its division, it becomes vectorizable.

       Deliberately declined, all verified in the testsuite: constant
       divisors (expand_divmod already does better), loops whose
       maximum trip count is below 3 (the extra preheader division does
       not amortise), targets without a highpart multiply or CLZ for
       the mode, types whose doubled precision has no real integer mode
       (so the transform can never expand into a libgcc call), and
       statements that can throw internally.  Two divisions by the same
       divisor share one magic computation via CSE.

=====================================================================
VALIDATION
=====================================================================

Everything below compares against a control compiler that differs only
by -fno-tree-loop-invdiv, so no result is confounded by the rest of the
patchset.

  gcc.c-torture/execute      1559 pass, 11 fail - all 11 verified
                             pre-existing (they fail identically on the
                             control; they are sandbox artifacts)
  gcc.dg/vect                1169 pass, 11 fail - all 11 pre-existing
  ICE and IL-verification sweep at -O3 -march=native -fchecking=2 over
  gcc.dg + gcc.dg/tree-ssa + gcc.dg/torture + c-torture/compile:
                             14,069 files, ZERO ICEs, ZERO
                             verify_gimple / verify_ssa failures

  Graphite changes were validated separately with
  -O3 -march=native -floop-nest-optimize -fgraphite-identity:
  gcc.dg/graphite 86 pass, gcc.c-torture/execute 1559 pass,
  gcc.dg/vect 1169 pass - 2814 executed tests, zero new failures.

  The patched tree was also configured and built from this patch alone
  (--with-isl=/usr, so Graphite is genuinely exercised) and the
  performance numbers above were reproduced with that compiler.

Two findings worth recording because they are the kind of thing that
quietly ships otherwise:

  * gcc.dg/tree-ssa/loadpre4.c looks like a regression and is not.  The
    two binaries are byte-identical (same md5sum, same asm) and the
    test segfaults nondeterministically either way.  It is a
    dg-do compile test with a bogus main(int *, int) signature that a
    run-everything harness wrongly executes.

  * An earlier revision of the invdiv pass retargeted the final
    statement's LHS to avoid a copy.  -fchecking=2 correctly rejected
    it ("type mismatch in rshift_expr") because that silently changes
    the statement's result type.  Removed; copy propagation deletes the
    copy anyway.  A release-build-only test run would have shipped it.

Regression tests added by this patchset:
  gcc.dg/tree-ssa/ms178-invdiv-1.c   scan: fires on unsigned and signed
                                     div/mod; declines constant divisor
                                     and a two-iteration loop
  gcc.dg/tree-ssa/ms178-invdiv-2.c   runtime: d == 1, d == -1, powers of
                                     two, type extremes, and guarded
                                     division by zero
  gcc.dg/graphite/ms178-isl-error-reset.c
  gcc.target/i386/ms178-fp-clamp-1.c
  gcc.dg/vect/ms178-vect-bitfield-op-1.c
  gcc.dg/vect/ms178-scev-rshift-1.c
